    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d294">

                <pb facs="0674" n="647" xml:id="pg647"/>

                <head>294. National Security Action Memorandum No. 195<anchor n="0" xml:id="d294-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-10-22">October 22,
                        1962</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list type="to">
                    <head>MEMORANDUM FOR</head>
                    <item>THE SECRETARY OF STATE</item>
                    <item>THE SECRETARY OF DEFENSE</item>
                    <item>THE ADMINISTRATOR, AID</item>
                    <item>THE DIRECTOR OF CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE</item>
                    <item>THE DIRECTOR, USIA</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>U.S. Policy Toward Indonesia</item>
                </list>
                <p>The President has considered the Plan of Action for Indonesia contained in the
                    memorandum from the Department of State dated October 10, 1962<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d294-f2"/> in response to NSAM 179 of August 18, 1962.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d294-f3"/> The President has approved those action recommendations
                    for emergency assistance proposed in the Plan of Action and the background
                    memorandum attached thereto with the exception, however, of the recommendation
                    that the United States supply $15 to $20 million of spare parts and raw
                    materials.</p>
                <p>The President wishes to be informed of the extent to which procurement of spare
                    parts and raw materials is proposed to take place outside the United States.</p>
                <p>The President has not approved the measures proposed beyond the emergency
                    assistance referred to above. He will, however, consider these and other
                    specific proposals for additional steps which the U.S. might take both in the
                    economic and diplomatic fields when they are presented.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">McGeorge Bundy</hi><anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d294-f4"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
